      "bestPractices": "# Newsletter best practices\n\nA sourced checklist of current tactics for writing an email newsletter. Use it as a pre-flight\ncheck, not a recipe — the house voice (first-person, plain, concrete) wins over any single rule.\n\n## The subject line\n\n- [ ] Earn the open with something specific and true. Front-load the message: most opens happen\n      on a phone, where mobile inboxes show only the first ~33–43 characters before truncating.\n- [ ] Keep it short. Mailchimp caps at 9 words / 60 characters; 30–50 characters is the widely\n      cited sweet spot, and the shortest lines (often 2–4 words) tend to test best.\n- [ ] At most one emoji, and use it to support a word, not replace it — it renders differently\n      across systems. No more than three punctuation marks; punctuation walls read as spam.\n- [ ] Don't title it \"Issue #11\" or \"This month's newsletter\". Name the one thing inside.\n\n## The preheader\n\n- [ ] Write the preheader on purpose. If you leave it blank, the client grabs the first text it\n      finds — alt text, a nav link, the unsubscribe line — almost never the teaser you want.\n- [ ] Extend the subject line, don't repeat it. If the subject teases, let the preheader explain;\n      if the subject explains, let the preheader tease. They're a two-part hook.\n- [ ] Front-load it: put the essential words in the first ~40 characters, since mobile clients\n      often show only 30–40 and Gmail's app truncates near 35–40.\n\n## The open\n\n- [ ] Land the one idea in the first sentence or two. Readers fixate on the headline and the\n      first line or two of a story, then scan; the primacy effect means the top is best retained.\n- [ ] No \"Welcome back to another edition of…\", no \"in this issue we'll cover…\". Just start.\n\n## One idea, scannable\n\n- [ ] One main thing per issue. Curated links can follow, but the issue needs a spine — a single\n      story or insight, often 300–600 words, beats a grab-bag of five tips for holding attention.\n- [ ] People give a newsletter about 51 seconds after opening — roughly 200 words — and most\n      scan rather than read. Write so the gist survives a scan.\n- [ ] Most readers scan in an F-pattern: they read across the top, drop down, read a shorter line,\n      then run down the left edge. Put the load-bearing words at the starts of lines and sections.\n- [ ] Give each chunk one point and a meaningful subheading; subheadings are the most effective\n      thing a scanner follows (the \"layer-cake\" pattern). Bold a few keywords so the bolded text\n      alone tells a coherent story.\n- [ ] Short paragraphs and single-idea bullets. In a bulleted list, the first item and the first\n      words of each bullet get read most — front-load there too.\n- [ ] Conversational but tight. Read it aloud; cut anything you'd never say to one person.\n\n## The CTA\n\n- [ ] One clear next step per issue — read, try, reply — not five competing asks. The inverted\n      triangle (broad open narrowing to one action) is the layout built for a single CTA.\n- [ ] Don't bury the CTA at the very bottom; many scanners never reach it. Keep button copy to\n      two to five words.\n- [ ] A genuine \"hit reply and tell me…\" both invites a real response and signals engagement to\n      the mailbox provider. Ask because you want the answer, not as bait.\n\n## Sender & format\n\n- [ ] Use a recognizable, consistent from-name. Recognizing the sender is the single biggest\n      reason people open — for ~88% of users it's the top factor, ahead of the subject line.\n      \"Sarah from Acme\" adds a human touch once the brand is known; otherwise lead with the brand.\n- [ ] Default to a clean, near-plain-text feel. In HubSpot's A/B tests, plain-text consistently\n      beat HTML on opens and clicks — adding images cut clickthrough, and the more HTML-rich the\n      email, the lower the open rate. Image-heavy templates also get filtered to Gmail's\n      Promotions tab.\n- [ ] Keep a healthy text-to-image ratio; a wall of images with little text trips spam filters\n      and breaks when a client blocks images by default.\n\n## Sources\n\n- Mailchimp — Best Practices for Email Subject Lines: https://mailchimp.com/help/best-practices-for-email-subject-lines/\n- Mailchimp — Email Sender Name Best Practices: https://mailchimp.com/resources/sender-name/\n- Twilio — What's the ideal email subject line length?: https://www.twilio.com/en-us/blog/ideal-email-subject-length\n- Campaign Monitor — Structure your email marketing for scanners: https://www.campaignmonitor.com/blog/email-marketing/structure-email-marketing-campaigns-scanners/\n- Nielsen Norman Group — Text Scanning Patterns: Eyetracking Evidence: https://www.nngroup.com/articles/text-scanning-patterns-eyetracking/\n- HubSpot — Plain Text vs. HTML Emails: Which Is Better? [New Data]: https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/plain-text-vs-html-emails-data\n- Litmus — State of Email Reports: https://www.litmus.com/state-of-email-reports\n- Substack/Kit — How to Write a Newsletter: https://kit.com/resources/blog/how-to-write-an-email\n",
      "specs": "# Email newsletter specs\n\nConcrete limits and specs for quick reference. Only the numbers below are sourced; don't add\nothers without a source. Display limits vary by client, so these are practical ranges, not\nguarantees — when it matters, preview in Litmus or Email on Acid.\n\n## Subject line\n\n| Thing | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Mailchimp recommended max | 9 words / 60 characters |\n| Widely cited sweet spot | 30–50 characters |\n| Shown on mobile before truncation | ~33–43 characters |\n| Shown on desktop (Gmail) | up to ~70 characters |\n| Max emojis | 1 |\n| Max punctuation marks | 3 |\n\nFront-load the message inside the first ~33 characters so it survives mobile truncation. Open\nrates drop as length grows, with the steepest fall after ~50 characters.\n\n## Preheader / preview text\n\n| Thing | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Recommended total length | 40–130 characters |\n| Shown on mobile before truncation | ~30–40 characters |\n| Shown on Gmail desktop | ~100–120 characters |\n| Essential words within first | ~40 characters |\n\nAlways set it explicitly; an empty preheader lets the client pull whatever text comes first in\nthe body. Extend the subject line, don't repeat it.\n\n## From-name\n\n- Recognizable and consistent across issues. Recognizing the sender is the top open factor for\n  ~88% of users — above subject-line relevance.\n- \"FirstName from Brand\" adds a human tone when the brand is already known; otherwise lead with\n  the brand name.\n\n## Layout & CTA\n\n| Thing | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Container width (desktop) | ~600px, single column |\n| Body font size | 16–18px |\n| CTA buttons per issue | 1 primary |\n| CTA copy length | 2–5 words |\n\nRead time after open averages ~51 seconds (~200 words), and most readers scan rather than read.\nDefault to a clean, near-plain-text feel: in HubSpot's A/B tests plain-text beat HTML on opens\nand clicks, and a single image reduced click rate. Always pair any HTML with a plain-text\n(multipart) version.\n\n## Sources\n\n- Mailchimp — Best Practices for Email Subject Lines: https://mailchimp.com/help/best-practices-for-email-subject-lines/\n- Mailchimp — Email Sender Name Best Practices: https://mailchimp.com/resources/sender-name/\n- Twilio — What's the ideal email subject line length?: https://www.twilio.com/en-us/blog/ideal-email-subject-length\n- Campaign Monitor — Structure your email marketing for scanners: https://www.campaignmonitor.com/blog/email-marketing/structure-email-marketing-campaigns-scanners/\n- Nielsen Norman Group — Text Scanning Patterns: Eyetracking Evidence: https://www.nngroup.com/articles/text-scanning-patterns-eyetracking/\n- HubSpot — Plain Text vs. HTML Emails: Which Is Better? [New Data]: https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/plain-text-vs-html-emails-data\n"
