      "bestPractices": "# X (Twitter) best practices\n\nA sourced checklist of current tactics for writing on X. Use it as a pre-flight check, not a\nrecipe — the house voice (first-person, plain, concrete) wins over any single rule.\n\n## The hook\n\n- [ ] The first line earns the stop. Open on the surprising claim, the number, the contradiction,\n      or the visceral moment. No throat-clearing, no \"A thread on…\".\n- [ ] Create an open question in the reader's mind — but don't give away the full answer in line one.\n- [ ] A hook is not clickbait: it promises only value the post actually delivers. Test it —\n      would *you* stop scrolling, and feel satisfied after?\n- [ ] Emotional or numeric specificity beats vague inspiration every time.\n- [ ] On X the preview shows ~280 characters; if your hook isn't in those first characters,\n      most people never tap to read more.\n\n## Length & format\n\n- [ ] One idea per post. Free accounts cap at 280 characters; if you have more, write a thread.\n- [ ] Keep posts tight. Sprout finds the 240–259 character band tends to earn the most\n      engagement; shorter, punchier posts also travel well. Cut every word that isn't load-bearing.\n- [ ] Text-based posts perform well on X — Buffer's analysis found them ahead of video, image,\n      and (especially) link-only posts for engagement. Don't add media just to add media.\n- [ ] When you do use media, native image or video lifts engagement; X is pushing video-first,\n      so prefer native video over a link to YouTube.\n\n## Links\n\n- [ ] External links suppress reach (Sprout cites 50–90% reductions). When a post needs a link,\n      put it in the first reply and say so, rather than in the main post.\n- [ ] Auto-shortened URLs always count as 23 characters regardless of real length.\n- [ ] Space links out — clustering many link posts in a short window lowers click-through per link.\n\n## Hashtags\n\n- [ ] 0–2 real hashtags, max. 1–2 can lift engagement; 3+ tends to drop it. No hashtag walls.\n\n## Engagement & cadence\n\n- [ ] The algorithm rewards fast early engagement and prioritizes replies — open questions and\n      genuine conversation starters beat like-bait.\n- [ ] No engagement bait (\"RT if you agree\", \"read that again\", \"let that sink in\"). It reads as\n      spam and undercuts the voice, whatever old \"ask for the retweet\" advice says.\n- [ ] Consistency beats bursts: a sustainable daily cadence (Buffer suggests ~3–5 posts/day for\n      growth-focused accounts) outperforms posting hard for two weeks and burning out.\n- [ ] Engagement skews to weekday mornings (Buffer: ~9–11 AM, Tue–Thu strongest), but post when\n      *your* audience is active. This is a scheduling note, not a drafting rule.\n\n## Threads\n\n- [ ] The first post is a hook, not a table of contents — it must stand on its own and earn the tap.\n- [ ] One beat per post; each should be worth reading even out of context.\n- [ ] Add small \"expansion hooks\" every few posts to keep people scrolling.\n- [ ] Number posts (`1/`, `2/`) only when it genuinely aids a longer thread; don't force it.\n- [ ] Close with a payoff or one clear takeaway — not \"follow me for more\".\n\n## X Premium\n\n- Premium accounts can post up to 25,000 characters and get a reach boost, but long posts still\n  show only a ~280-character preview in-feed — so the hook discipline above still applies. This is\n  context, not a drafting target: default to writing as if the reader is on a free account unless\n  told otherwise.\n\n## Sources\n\n- HubSpot — How to Market on X (formerly Twitter): https://blog.hubspot.com/blog/tabid/6307/bid/25084/the-ultimate-cheat-sheet-for-expert-twitter-marketing.aspx\n- HubSpot — Twitter for Business: https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/twitter-for-business\n- Sprout Social — How the Twitter (X) Algorithm Works in 2026: https://sproutsocial.com/insights/twitter-algorithm/\n- Sprout Social — How long should social posts be? (character counter): https://sproutsocial.com/insights/social-media-character-counter/\n- Buffer — Good Hooks: How to Grab and Keep Attention: https://buffer.com/resources/good-hooks/\n- Buffer — Best Time to Post on Twitter/X in 2026: https://buffer.com/resources/best-time-to-post-on-twitter-x/\n- Buffer — How the Twitter (Now X) Algorithm Works in 2026: https://buffer.com/resources/twitter-timeline-algorithm/\n- Buffer — A Scientific Guide to Writing Great Tweets: https://buffer.com/resources/writing-great-tweets-scientific-guide/\n",
